---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PlaceholderImageGenerator from '@/components/tools/generators/PlaceholderImageGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="placeholder-image-generator">
  <PlaceholderImageGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to make a placeholder image</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Enter the size, or pick a preset: Open Graph, hero, thumbnail, avatar and the standard ad slots.</li>
      <li>Set the colours. The label stays legible automatically if you press “Auto” next to the text colour.</li>
      <li>Leave the grid on if you want it obvious at a glance that the image is a stand-in.</li>
      <li>Choose PNG, WebP or SVG, then download the file or copy the data URI straight into your markup.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why not use a placeholder service</h2>
    <p>
      Hosted placeholder image services are convenient, but each image is a request to somebody
      else’s server, so:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your prototype gets slower. A grid of twenty placeholders is twenty DNS lookups, TLS handshakes and round trips.</li>
      <li>Your work leaks. The referrer header tells the service which page (often on a staging domain) loads the image.</li>
      <li>Layouts break without warning. When the service is down, rate-limits you, or quietly changes its URL format, every mockup in the project breaks at once.</li>
      <li>They do not work offline: on a plane, in a client meeting, behind a strict firewall.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A generated file or an inline data URI has none of those problems, costs nothing, and looks
      exactly the same every time.
    </p>

    <h2>Which format to pick</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Format</th>
            <th>Typical size</th>
            <th>Scales</th>
            <th>Best for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>SVG</td><td>Under 1 KB</td><td>Infinitely</td><td>Almost everything: inline data URIs, responsive layouts, design handoff</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PNG</td><td>5–60 KB</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Email templates, older tooling, anything that refuses SVG</td></tr>
          <tr><td>WebP</td><td>25–35% smaller than PNG</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Web mockups where file size matters and support is guaranteed</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      SVG is best for a placeholder, because the content is a rectangle, a few lines and some text:
      the exact shapes vectors are made for. The result is a few hundred bytes at any dimension, and
      it stays sharp on a 3× display without a second file.
    </p>

    <h2>Standard image sizes worth remembering</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Use</th>
            <th>Size</th>
            <th>Ratio</th>
            <th>Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Open Graph / social share</td><td>1200 × 630</td><td>1.91:1</td><td>Facebook, LinkedIn, Slack, Discord, WhatsApp</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Twitter summary_large_image</td><td>1200 × 675</td><td>16:9</td><td>Also crops acceptably at 1.91:1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Instagram feed</td><td>1080 × 1080</td><td>1:1</td><td>Portrait 1080 × 1350 gets more screen space</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Full-width hero</td><td>1920 × 1080</td><td>16:9</td><td>Serve a 960 px version to mobile</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Card thumbnail</td><td>400 × 300</td><td>4:3</td><td>Common in listing grids</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Avatar</td><td>256 × 256</td><td>1:1</td><td>Rendered anywhere from 24 to 128 px</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Leaderboard ad</td><td>728 × 90</td><td>8.09:1</td><td>IAB standard</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Medium rectangle ad</td><td>300 × 250</td><td>1.2:1</td><td>IAB standard, the most widely bought slot</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Data URIs: when they help and when they hurt</h2>
    <p>
      A data URI embeds the whole file in the document as Base64 text. That removes a network request,
      which helps for a tiny image needed at once. But it has costs that are easy to miss:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Roughly 33% larger. Base64 spends four characters on every three bytes.</li>
      <li>No separate caching. The image is re-downloaded with the HTML on every uncached page view, and cannot be shared between pages.</li>
      <li>Parser cost. A very long data URI in the markup is bytes the HTML parser must chew through before it can continue.</li>
      <li>Unreadable diffs. A 40 KB string in a component file makes code review miserable.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The practical line is a few kilobytes. Under that, inline it. Above roughly 30 KB, save the
      file and link to it. The tool warns you when the URI is above that size. SVG stays
      well under the limit at any dimension, which is another reason to prefer it here.
    </p>

    <h2>Reserving space and avoiding layout shift</h2>
    <p>
      Placeholders exist to prove a layout works, so use them the way the real images will be used:
      always give the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> explicit <code>width</code> and <code>height</code>
      attributes. Modern browsers use the ratio between them to reserve the correct box before the
      file has loaded, which is the difference between a page that settles smoothly and one that
      jumps. The copyable <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> tag above already includes both.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
